            <ArticleTitle>Conscious pain mapping.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>587-90</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To present the technique and usefulness of conscious pain mapping.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Prospective, observational study (Canadian Task Force classification II-2).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Gynecology departments of a university-affiliated hospital and a private community hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Fifty consecutive women undergoing diagnostic microlaparoscopy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Conscious pain mapping was performed in all 50 women.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MEASUREMENTS AND MAIN RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Conscious pain mapping helped to identify foci of chronic pelvic pain. The appendix and pelvic adhesions accounted for a significant amount of pelvic pain in these women.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Conscious pain mapping helps to uncover sites of pelvic pain that might not be identified during traditional laparoscopy under general anesthesia.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
